WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.161
Upload entry points are not limited to a single browser form.

2
00:00:04.161 --> 00:00:06.139
Files can arrive through ASP.

3
00:00:06.139 --> 00:00:16.302
NET Core MVC pages, minimal APIs, Web APIs, multipart requests, administrative tools, scheduled imports, partner integrations, and support workflows.

4
00:00:16.302 --> 00:00:24.350
A secure design identifies every entry point and applies consistent rules where the same business process is involved.

5
00:00:24.350 --> 00:00:26.260
Limits should be deliberate.

6
00:00:26.260 --> 00:00:39.356
Request size limits, form limits, multipart limits, file count limits, endpoint-specific limits, and business limits help protect the application from accidental overload and unclear behavior.

7
00:00:39.356 --> 00:00:49.724
A profile photo, invoice attachment, bulk import, and administrative report upload may each need different limits because they serve different purposes.

8
00:00:49.724 --> 00:00:52.998
Streaming versus buffering is a design decision.

9
00:00:52.998 --> 00:01:00.091
Buffering may be simpler for small files, but it can put pressure on memory, disk, or temporary storage.

10
00:01:00.091 --> 00:01:09.777
Streaming can support larger files and early processing, but it requires careful validation, cancellation, error handling, and storage design.

11
00:01:09.777 --> 00:01:16.598
Choose based on expected size, performance needs, validation requirements, and operational capacity.

12
00:01:16.598 --> 00:01:19.531
Interrupted uploads should fail gracefully.

13
00:01:19.531 --> 00:01:27.579
Timeouts and cancellation tokens help the application stop work when a client disconnects or a request exceeds policy.

14
00:01:27.579 --> 00:01:38.970
Upload code should avoid continuing expensive processing after the caller is gone and should clean up partial temporary files when the workflow cannot safely continue.

15
00:01:38.970 --> 00:01:44.222
Early rejection is useful when a request is clearly unsupported or oversized.

16
00:01:44.222 --> 00:01:54.522
The application should reject files that do not match the endpoint purpose, exceed limits, or violate basic business rules before doing expensive work.

17
00:01:54.522 --> 00:02:06.253
That said, early rejection is only the first layer; accepted files still need storage isolation, validation, scanning or review where appropriate, and authorization checks.

18
00:02:06.253 --> 00:02:11.369
Avoid unbounded reads and memory-heavy processing of untrusted upload data.

19
00:02:11.369 --> 00:02:22.282
A safe upload handler controls how much it reads, where data is buffered, how temporary files are managed, and what happens when validation or processing fails.

20
00:02:22.282 --> 00:02:29.854
Those controls should apply consistently across public endpoints, internal workflows, and administrative paths.
